WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:07.562
The payment service needs to call the card issuer at issuer.example.com.

2
00:00:07.562 --> 00:00:10.600
But a packet needs an IP address, not a name.

3
00:00:10.600 --> 00:00:12.896
Something has to look the name up.

4
00:00:13.100 --> 00:00:17.997
The service asks the stub resolver, a small piece of code on its own machine.

5
00:00:17.997 --> 00:00:19.715
The stub does no searching.

6
00:00:19.715 --> 00:00:23.531
It forwards the question to a recursive resolver, and waits.

7
00:00:23.733 --> 00:00:26.287
The recursive resolver does the searching.

8
00:00:26.287 --> 00:00:30.787
Its cache is empty, so it starts at the top of the tree, at a root server.

9
00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:33.247
The root doesn't know the address.

10
00:00:33.247 --> 00:00:38.864
It knows who runs each top-level domain, and refers the resolver to the .com servers.

11
00:00:39.067 --> 00:00:42.932
A .com server knows who runs each name under .com.

12
00:00:42.932 --> 00:00:46.952
It refers the resolver to example.com's own servers.

13
00:00:47.167 --> 00:00:49.703
Those are the authoritative servers.

14
00:00:49.703 --> 00:00:51.746
They hold the actual records.

15
00:00:51.746 --> 00:00:59.496
One answers with the address, and a time to live, or TTL, of 300 seconds: how long anyone may keep the answer.

16
00:00:59.700 --> 00:01:04.760
The resolver hands the address back to the stub, and the payment service can connect.

17
00:01:04.760 --> 00:01:08.033
That took four round trips, here about 60 milliseconds.

18
00:01:08.267 --> 00:01:13.052
On the way, the resolver cached every answer, each with its own TTL.

19
00:01:13.267 --> 00:01:16.595
A second later, the next payment needs the same name.

20
00:01:16.595 --> 00:01:20.991
The resolver finds it in its cache and answers in about a millisecond.

21
00:01:20.991 --> 00:01:22.247
No trip up the tree.

22
00:01:22.467 --> 00:01:23.993
The cached answer ages.

23
00:01:23.993 --> 00:01:27.445
When its 300 seconds run out, the resolver drops it.

24
00:01:27.445 --> 00:01:34.348
The next lookup goes straight to the example.com servers, because the resolver still knows who they are.

25
00:01:34.567 --> 00:01:37.492
That's why a DNS change is never instant.

26
00:01:37.492 --> 00:01:42.559
Until the old TTL runs out, resolvers keep handing out the old address.

27
00:01:42.767 --> 00:01:51.093
So a lookup walks down the tree, from the root to .com to example.com, and every answer is cached for its TTL.
